Chapter: HdaPlayGated
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Kernel chapter HdaAudio

 codex-vm counts every PCM buffer it drains to the DAC and exposes the
 running total at HDA #180, which is the only way a headless test can tell
 that a sound was actually submitted.

 Two things are asserted and the second is the one worth having. The open
 gate proves `hda-play-pcm-at` streams from a descriptor list and a sample
 buffer the CALLER owns and reuses, which is what a caller whose heap is
 rewound every frame needs. The shut gate proves a refusal submits nothing:
 a mute that still reached the controller would drain buffers silently and
 look exactly like a mute that worked, because nobody in a headless run can
 hear either one.

Section: Body

  plays : Integer = 5
  samples : Integer = 480

  play-if : Boolean, Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  play-if (on) (bdl) (buf) =
    if on then hda-play-pcm-at bdl buf (samples * 4) else 0

  play-loop : Boolean, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Audio] Integer
  play-loop (on) (bdl) (buf) (i) =
    if i >= plays then 0
    else act
      p <- play-if on bdl buf
      rest <- play-loop on bdl buf (i + 1)
      p + rest
    end

  opening : [Console, Audio, Device.Mmio] Nothing = act
    let bdl = alloc-bytes 16
    in let buf = hda-square-tone 880 samples
    in act
      before <- hda-drained-count
      a <- play-loop True bdl buf 0
      opened <- hda-drained-count
      b <- play-loop False bdl buf 0
      shut <- hda-drained-count
      print-line-uni ("before=" & show before & " open=" & show opened & " shut=" & show shut)
      if opened - before /= plays then print-line-uni ("FAIL: open gate drained " & show (opened - before))
      else if shut /= opened then print-line-uni ("FAIL: shut gate drained " & show (shut - opened))
      else print-line-uni "PASS"
    end
  end

